    "body": "Findings from the second worker run (gate attempt, no push):\n\nThe held work is committed on `issue/2774-rotary-heights` (8f6e59d1e) in worktree `.claude/worktrees/issue-2774`. The quick gate ran once to completion: 31 of 33 steps ok, one red step - `motion suites: rotary`, one red test: `a_band_that_wraps_the_whole_skin_adds_no_links` (hop link count 276 with the wrapping band r0..r8.5, 88 without). A second gate run was voided by a mid-run edit; the fix attempt after it made two more band tests red, so the fix was reverted and the tree stands again at the committed state.\n\nWhat was learned:\n\n- The comb is not only a twisted-part cost: on the smooth turned bar, the trim's per-chord span solve still breaks some pass glues near the axis. The tip read `hypot(y, z + r) - r` goes NEGATIVE where the ball dips inside the axis cylinder, and the span guards (`ra > lo`) then drop those chord halves. With `bottom = 0.0` the dropped halves chop the run, and the relink turns each chop into a retract, index and plunge.\n- Two attempted fixes failed and were reverted: (1) clamping the read at 0.0 - `max(0.0)` makes flat regions that the ternary search and the bisect guards handle wrong; (2) rewriting the convex-span guards - mathematically it looked equivalent, but `the_parallel_band_cuts_between_two_cylinders` and the cross-no-skin refusal went red with it, so the pinned behaviour depends on the exact guard shapes and the convention needs a re-read, not a patch.\n- The band code was split into `multiaxis/radial_band.rs` at HEAD (400-line law); the split is mechanical and the tree passes clippy `-D warnings` on `camcraft-motion` and `camcraft-app`.\n\nNext worker needs to: read the tip convention against the test's rho (`hypot(y, z)` of the stored point) and the generator's ball-reach read side by side, then decide whether the wrap band should skip the trim entirely when the band covers the path's whole reach (min_r..max_r inside lo..hi -> return the base path unchanged). That early-out would fix the wrap case without touching the span law.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
